---
/**
 * /handoff/emails — the viewer. One email, centred, on a neutral grey, with the controls above it.
 *
 * WHAT IT IS FOR, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE SECTION ON `/handoff/`. That section is the OVERVIEW: three
 * emails at once, each beside its subject line, its variable list and its plain-text part, so a
 * reader can compare them and see what the deliverable contains. This page is the opposite job —
 * look at ONE of them properly, at a chosen width, with nothing else on screen. Both exist because
 * neither can do the other's job without becoming worse at its own: an overview that hands one email
 * the whole viewport stops being an overview, and a viewer carrying three variable lists stops being
 * a viewer.
 *
 * ── THE SURROUND IS NEUTRAL NO MATTER WHAT THEME THE PORTAL IS IN, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ───────
 * Every other page in this app follows `data-theme`. This one must not. A mail client's chrome is
 * not ours, and a dark backdrop behind a white email tints the judgement being made about the email:
 * the same greys read heavier, the card edge reads as a glow, and the reviewer decides something
 * about a design that only the backdrop was doing. So the page imports NO stylesheet — not the brand
 * bridge, not the portal sheet — and declares its own literal greys. That is a stronger guarantee
 * than omitting the theme-stamping script would be: with no `--bo-*` token anywhere on the page,
 * there is nothing for a theme to reach.
 *
 * ── EVERY EMAIL SHIPS AND THE QUERY PICKS ONE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A static build cannot vary on a query parameter, so all three frames are in the HTML and
 * `lib/email-viewer.ts` reveals one. The URL carries both the email and the width, and every toggle
 * writes it back, so the address bar always names what is on screen — the portal's own rule. What
 * that costs is stated in the controller: `smoke-support` requesting a variant proves the ROUTE
 * serves, never that the variant renders.
 *
 * ── THE FOUR EXCLUSIONS, EACH CLOSED THE WAY `/handoff/` CLOSES IT ─────────────────────────────
 *   header nav      never added to `components/Header.astro`'s NAV list
 *   docs sidebar    not a content-collection entry, so it never appears
 *   search          `lib/search-modal.ts` NOT_DOCS carries `handoff`, and its predicate matches the
 *                   FIRST SEGMENT — `/handoff/emails/` is already excluded by that one entry. Adding
 *                   `handoff/emails` would be a second rule for one fact.
 *   sitemap         `pruneSitemap` takes exact paths, so this one is named in `astro.config.mjs`
 *                   beside `/handoff/`, plus the `noindex` below. `noindex` asks a crawler not to
 *                   keep the page; a sitemap entry asks it to come and look. One without the other
 *                   says both.
 * And it is in `smoke-support.mjs` REQUIRED_ROUTES with its six variants: only `/handoff/` links
 * here, so a derived route list would lose it in silence if it ever stopped generating.
 *
 * ── NO PLAIN-TEXT PART ON THIS PAGE, AND THAT IS A DECISION ────────────────────────────────────
 * It is half the deliverable and it is not half of THIS page's job. A plain-text part has no width,
 * so neither control above it applies to it; putting it here would mean a block that ignores both
 * toggles, sitting under the one thing the page exists to show. It already has a home on `/handoff/`
 * beside each email's variables, and the footer here links back to it by name rather than leaving a
 * reader to wonder where it went.
 *
 * NO ASTRO COMMENT MAY GO INSIDE A TEMPLATE EXPRESSION BELOW — six SSR 500s with empty bodies are on
 * record from exactly that, and every gate passes them.
 */
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { EMAIL_PREVIEWS, EMAILS_NOT_BUILT } from '../../lib/email-previews';
import { WIDTHS } from '../../lib/email-viewer';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <title>Email viewer · Baseout Support</title>
  </head>

  <body>
    <div class="ev" data-viewer data-email={EMAIL_PREVIEWS[0]?.slug} data-style="one" data-width="desktop" data-scheme="light">
      <header class="ev-bar">
        <a class="ev-back" href="/handoff/#surface-emails">
          <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} />
          <span>Handoff</span>
        </a>

        <div class="ev-picks" role="group" aria-label="Which email">
          {EMAIL_PREVIEWS.map((mail) => (
            <button type="button" class="ev-pick" data-email-pick={mail.slug} aria-current="false">
              {mail.title}
            </button>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="ev-styles" role="group" aria-label="Style">
          <button type="button" class="ev-w" data-style-pick="one" aria-pressed="true">Style 1</button>
          <button type="button" class="ev-w" data-style-pick="two" aria-pressed="false">Style 2</button>
        </div>

        <div class="ev-schemes" role="group" aria-label="Palette">
          <button type="button" class="ev-w" data-scheme-pick="light" aria-pressed="true">
            <Icon name="sun" size={14} />
            <span>Light</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="ev-w" data-scheme-pick="dark" aria-pressed="false">
            <Icon name="moon" size={14} />
            <span>Dark</span>
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="ev-widths" role="group" aria-label="Width">
          <button type="button" class="ev-w" data-width-pick="desktop" aria-pressed="true">
            <Icon name="monitor" size={14} />
            <span>Desktop</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="ev-w" data-width-pick="mobile" aria-pressed="false">
            <Icon name="smartphone" size={14} />
            <span>Mobile</span>
          </button>
          <span class="ev-px" data-width-px>{WIDTHS.desktop}px</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      {EMAILS_NOT_BUILT && (
        <p class="ev-warn">
          <strong>The templates have not been compiled.</strong> Run
          <code>pnpm --filter @baseout/support emails</code> and build again.
        </p>
      )}

      <main class="ev-stage">
        {EMAIL_PREVIEWS.map((mail, i) =>
          (['one', 'two'] as const).map((style) => (
            <div class="ev-paper" data-hidden={String(i !== 0 || style !== 'one')}>
              {mail.styles[style].html ? (
                <iframe
                  class="ev-frame"
                  title={`${mail.title}, style ${style === 'one' ? '1' : '2'} — the rendered email`}
                  srcdoc={mail.styles[style].html}
                  sandbox="allow-same-origin"
                  data-em-frame={`${mail.slug}|${style}`}
                />
              ) : (
                <p class="ev-empty">Not compiled — run the build script.</p>
              )}
            </div>
          )),
        )}
      </main>

    </div>

    <script>
      import { wireEmailViewer } from '../../lib/email-viewer';
      wireEmailViewer();
    </script>

    <style is:global>
      /* ── LITERAL GREYS, NO TOKENS, NO THEME ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Read the frontmatter before changing any of these to a `var(--bo-*)`: the point of the page
         is that the surround cannot be tinted by the portal's theme. There is no brand colour here
         because there is no brand surface here — the only designed object on screen is inside the
         frame, and everything else is the table it is lying on. */
      :root {
        color-scheme: light;
      }
      * {
        box-sizing: border-box;
      }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        background: #e6e6e6;
        color: #111111;
        font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
      }

      /* THE STAGE IS THE WHOLE PAGE UNDER THE TOOLBAR. There was an explanatory footer here and it
         has moved to `/handoff/`, where the rest of the writing about these emails lives: this page
         is for looking at one artefact, and a page that ends in three paragraphs of ours is a page
         that ends in ours rather than in the email. With it gone the stage takes the remaining
         height, so the email's own ground runs to the bottom of the window at any size. */
      .ev {
        min-height: 100vh;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
      }

      /* The controls sit ABOVE the email and stay there while it scrolls — a toolbar you have to
         scroll back up to reach is a toolbar you stop using on a 2,000px email. */
      .ev-bar {
        position: sticky;
        top: 0;
        z-index: 2;
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 12px;
        padding: 12px 16px;
        background: #f2f2f2;
        border-bottom: 1px solid #cccccc;
      }
      .ev-bar > * {
        margin-top: 0;
      }
      .ev-back {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 4px;
        color: #555555;
        font-size: 13px;
        text-decoration: none;
      }
      .ev-back:hover {
        color: #111111;
      }

      /* THE EMAIL PICKER IS FIRST AND THE WIDTH IS LAST, in that order, because they answer
         different questions: which document am I looking at, then how am I looking at it. The width
         pushes to the right edge so the two groups cannot be mistaken for one row of six. */
      .ev-picks {
        display: flex;
        gap: 4px;
      }
      /* THE PALETTE AND THE WIDTH ARE ONE CLUSTER, pushed right, because both answer "how am I
         looking at it" while the picker on the left answers "at what". The auto margin moved from
         the width group to the palette group when the palette arrived; leaving it on both is how a
         toolbar ends up with a gap nobody chose. */
      /* THE PICKER ANSWERS "WHICH EMAIL"; THE THREE GROUPS ON THE RIGHT ANSWER "DRAWN WHICH WAY,
         IN WHICH PALETTE, AT WHICH WIDTH". The auto margin lives on the first of the right-hand
         groups and nowhere else; putting it on more than one is how a toolbar ends up with a gap
         nobody chose. */
      .ev-styles {
        display: flex;
        gap: 4px;
        margin-left: auto;
      }
      .ev-schemes {
        display: flex;
        gap: 4px;
      }
      .ev-widths {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 4px;
      }

      .ev-pick,
      .ev-w {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 4px;
        padding: 4px 12px;
        border: 1px solid #cccccc;
        border-radius: 8px;
        background: #ffffff;
        color: #333333;
        font: inherit;
        font-size: 13px;
        cursor: pointer;
      }
      .ev-pick:hover,
      .ev-w:hover {
        border-color: #999999;
        color: #111111;
      }
      /* THE SELECTED STATE IS THE ARIA STATE. `aria-current` for the email — one of three is the
         document you are on — and `aria-pressed` for the width, which is a mode being held. Carrying
         either as a class as well is how the two facts drift apart. */
      .ev-pick[aria-current='true'],
      .ev-w[aria-pressed='true'] {
        background: #111111;
        border-color: #111111;
        color: #ffffff;
      }
      .ev-px {
        min-width: 48px;
        font-size: 12px;
        color: #666666;
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      }

      .ev-warn {
        margin: 16px;
        padding: 12px;
        border-radius: 8px;
        background: #ffffff;
        border: 1px solid #cccccc;
        font-size: 13px;
      }
      .ev-warn code {
        font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
        font-size: 12px;
      }

      /* Grey around, the email in the middle. The stage is what makes the email an object on a
         surface rather than a page that happens to start at the left margin.

         AND THE STAGE FOLLOWS THE EMAIL'S PALETTE, which reverses what this file used to say. The
         old rule was that the surround stays a fixed neutral so the portal's theme cannot tint the
         judgement, and that was right while the email had one palette. It has two now, and a light
         stage behind a `#0a0a0a` email is not neutral: it is a 15:1 frame around the artefact, which
         tints the judgement harder than the portal ever did. A real client paints its chrome and its
         message from the same theme, so the stage does too. The TOOLBAR deliberately does not — it
         is an instrument, not part of the picture, and a control that restyles itself while you are
         comparing two renderings is one more variable in the comparison. */
      .ev-stage {
        flex: 1 1 auto;
        display: flex;
        justify-content: center;
        /* NO HORIZONTAL PADDING AT DESKTOP, and no cap on the pane. Both were leaving a strip of
           stage down each side, which put a second frame around an email whose own ground is the
           frame. The pane runs the full width of the window, exactly as a reading pane does; the
           mobile rule below puts the padding back, because there the frame is an object and needs
           room around it. */
        padding: 0;
        background: #e6e6e6;
        /* The stage colour is now only ever seen AROUND the 375px frame. At desktop the email's own
           ground covers the pane, which is the point of the change above. */
        /* The 375px frame must not be squeezed below its own width on a narrow screen: the width IS
           the thing being shown, so the surround scrolls instead. */
        overflow-x: auto;
      }
      /* ── THE FRAME IS A READING PANE, NOT A PICTURE FRAME ────────────────────────────────────
         This used to be a bordered 600px card with `overflow: hidden`, and it cut the email off at
         exactly the width of its own body. That is not what any mail client does: a client gives the
         message a pane as wide as the window, the email's `mj-body` background fills the whole pane,
         and MJML's 600px container sits centred inside it. Cropping to 600 hid the one thing the
         ground is for, which is being the surround.

         So DESKTOP is now full width and the email supplies its own background edge to edge, exactly
         as it will in the pane. MOBILE stays pinned to 375px, because there the number IS the point:
         375 is what fires the breakpoints, and a full-width mobile frame would test nothing. */
      .ev-paper {
        flex: 1 1 auto;
        align-self: flex-start;
        width: 100%;
        overflow: hidden;
        background: #ffffff;
      }
      .ev[data-width='mobile'] .ev-stage {
        padding: 32px 16px 48px;
      }
      .ev[data-width='mobile'] .ev-paper {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        width: 375px;
        max-width: 375px;
        border-radius: 8px;
        /* Only the phone gets an edge: at 375 the frame is an object sitting on the stage, and
           without a border it would float with no indication of where the screen stops. */
        border: 1px solid #cccccc;
      }
      .ev[data-scheme='dark'][data-width='mobile'] .ev-paper {
        border-color: #3a3a3a;
      }
      /* A DATA ATTRIBUTE, NOT `hidden`: an author `display` beats that attribute, and `.ev-paper`
         sets one. This app has already shipped that defect once. */
      .ev-paper[data-hidden='true'] {
        display: none;
      }
      .ev[data-scheme='dark'] .ev-stage {
        background: #2b2b2b;
      }
      .ev[data-scheme='dark'] .ev-paper {
        border-color: #3a3a3a;
        background: #0a0a0a;
        box-shadow: none;
      }
      /* ── THE GROUND RUNS TO THE BOTTOM OF THE WINDOW, AND THE EMAIL PAINTS IT ─────────────────
         A short email used to end partway down and leave the stage's grey under it, which read as
         the email being cropped a second time. The fix is NOT to paint the stage the email's colour:
         that would hide the case where an email fails to set its own background, which is a real
         defect this page exists to catch. Instead the frame is given a floor of one viewport minus
         the 50px toolbar, and an iframe taller than its content has its canvas painted by the
         DOCUMENT'S OWN background. So the ground filling the window is the email's ground, proving
         itself, and `fitEmailFrame` still grows the frame past this for anything longer. */
      .ev-frame {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: 640px;
        min-height: calc(100vh - 50px);
        border: 0;
      }
      /* Not at 375: there the frame is a phone-shaped object on a stage, and stretching it to the
         window would draw a 375-wide column of email down a desktop screen, which is no device. */
      .ev[data-width='mobile'] .ev-frame {
        min-height: 0;
      }
      .ev-empty {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 24px;
        width: 100%;
        font-size: 13px;
        color: #666666;
      }



      @media (max-width: 640px) {
        .ev-bar {
          gap: 8px;
        }
        .ev-widths {
          margin-left: 0;
        }
        .ev-stage {
          padding: 16px 8px 32px;
        }
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
